Yeah. “Dragonfly see” is tracking the encounter in motion. What someone projects outward, what each person appraises it to mean, and what their reactions send back into the room. By the time a second person responds, they may be responding to the first person’s reaction as much as to the original words.

Take “You’ve been quiet today.”

1. What’s projected outward. The speaker might be checking in. Their tone might also carry some frustration.
2. What’s perceived. You hear concern. Someone across the table hears a public callout. A third person notices you stiffen and takes that as a sign there’s tension.
3. What changes. You say, “I’m fine,” a little sharply. The speaker now has new evidence to appraise and may conclude you’re upset with them. Their next words come from that updated read.
4. Where you can guide it. “I was thinking it through. Did you want my read?” gives everyone a little more information and gives the speaker a chance to say what they were reaching for.

The tricky part is a reaction can make an earlier guess come true later. Maybe the speaker was curious at first and got irritated after the sharp reply. If you look only at the end, you could say, “See, they were irritated,” and be right about what you’re seeing now. The sequence tells you when it started and what might have brought it on.

That’s what I’d keep for Pantheon. Chase the change. Check what caused it. Guide the next exchange with information that lets people revise their read. And follow your own effect on the room, too. Once you speak, you’re part of what everybody else is trying to see.